<PostLayout
  title="DITA from FrameMaker: Two Schools of Thought"
  date="2019"
  summary="Comparing two approaches to DITA conversion from unstructured FrameMaker, and why full conversion worked better for content reuse."
>

<p>
  When a customer required documentation source in DITA format alongside PDFs, with ongoing
  updates over two years, we had to decide how to handle the conversion from unstructured
  FrameMaker. Our research led to two distinct approaches.
</p>

<h2>The Two Approaches</h2>

<h3>Approach 1: Maintain FrameMaker, Export DITA Per Release</h3>

<ol>
  <li>Convert unstructured FrameMaker to structured FrameMaker</li>
  <li>Create mapping tables to convert structured FrameMaker to DITA each release</li>
  <li>Run FrameMaker scripts to clean up the DITA output</li>
</ol>

<p>A sister team within the organization followed this approach. It works well when the DITA
deliverable is purely a customer format requirement and there is no internal need for content reuse.</p>

<h3>Approach 2: Convert Fully to DITA</h3>

<ol>
  <li>Use a third-party migration service (Stilo Migrate) for the initial unstructured-to-DITA conversion</li>
  <li>Handle pre-conversion and post-conversion cleanup</li>
  <li>Continue using DITA as the primary authoring format for all subsequent work</li>
</ol>

<p>We chose this approach. Our internal content reuse requirement across product lines was the
primary driver.</p>

<h2>Why We Chose Full Conversion</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Content reuse</strong>: DITA's topic-based architecture enabled sharing content across product lines, which was impossible with unstructured FrameMaker</li>
  <li><strong>Long-term investment</strong>: The customer required two years of ongoing DITA updates. Per-release exports would mean running the conversion process every cycle</li>
  <li><strong>Single source of truth</strong>: Maintaining two parallel sources (FrameMaker + DITA) creates drift and quality risks</li>
</ul>

<h2>Challenges We Encountered</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Object management</strong>: The number of DITA topics and maps after conversion was significantly larger than the FrameMaker book structure. A CCMS would help at scale</li>
  <li><strong>Editor investment</strong>: We needed a DITA-aware XML editor. We continued using FrameMaker as the XML editor</li>
  <li><strong>Repository limitations</strong>: With Perforce, we could not selectively check out individual topics. A CCMS would solve this</li>
</ul>

<h2>How to Choose</h2>

<ul>
  <li>If DITA is purely a <strong>delivery format</strong> and you have no content reuse needs, Approach 1 (maintain FrameMaker, export per release) is simpler</li>
  <li>If you need <strong>content reuse across products</strong> or anticipate long-term DITA authoring, Approach 2 (full conversion) is the better investment despite higher upfront cost</li>
</ul>

<p>There is no universally correct approach. The decision depends on whether DITA is a delivery format or an authoring strategy.</p>

</PostLayout>
